/* Clinical Pharmacology, batch 3: mechanisms, adverse effects, interactions, special groups, monitoring, addiction, dementia, ADHD, hypnotics, NICE choices and history. Original cards. */
(window.PA_CARDS = window.PA_CARDS || {}).pharm = (window.PA_CARDS.pharm || []).concat([
/* ---------- Receptors and mechanisms ---------- */
["Receptors and mechanisms","Antidepressant mechanisms","How does duloxetine work?","It blocks reuptake of both serotonin and noradrenaline (an SNRI), slightly more strongly at the serotonin transporter","Raising both monoamines helps mood and pain; duloxetine is also licensed for diabetic neuropathic pain and generalised anxiety disorder."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Antidepressant mechanisms","How does reboxetine work?","Selective noradrenaline reuptake inhibitor (NRI)","It has little effect on serotonin, so serotonergic side effects such as nausea and sexual problems are uncommon."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Antidepressant mechanisms","A 58-year-old man on reboxetine has urinary hesitancy and a dry mouth. Which drug class can relieve this?","An alpha-1 blocker such as tamsulosin or doxazosin","These effects are noradrenergic, not anticholinergic: extra noradrenaline stimulates alpha-1 receptors at the bladder neck, and blocking them relaxes it."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Antidepressant mechanisms","What does 'NaSSA' stand for, and which drug is the example?","Noradrenergic and specific serotonergic antidepressant: mirtazapine","Alpha-2 blockade releases noradrenaline and serotonin, while 5-HT2 and 5-HT3 blockade means fewer sexual, sleep and gut side effects than SSRIs."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Antidepressant mechanisms","How does vortioxetine work?","It blocks serotonin reuptake and also acts on several serotonin receptors (for example 5-HT3 and 5-HT7 antagonism, 5-HT1A agonism)","It is called a multimodal antidepressant; nausea is its commonest side effect."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Antidepressant mechanisms","Which SSRI also weakly blocks dopamine reuptake?","Sertraline","This mild dopamine transporter action is unusual among SSRIs; its clinical importance is uncertain."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Antidepressant mechanisms","Which SSRI also blocks 5-HT2C receptors, which may explain why it feels activating?","Fluoxetine","5-HT2C blockade frees dopamine and noradrenaline release in the prefrontal cortex, which can energise or cause agitation and insomnia."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Antidepressant mechanisms","Which SSRI has the most anticholinergic action?","Paroxetine","It binds muscarinic receptors, so dry mouth and constipation are commoner than with other SSRIs."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Antidepressant mechanisms","How does oral selegiline work at its usual dose?","Selective, irreversible MAO-B inhibitor","At usual oral doses it is used in Parkinson's disease; at higher doses (such as the transdermal antidepressant form) it also blocks MAO-A."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Antidepressant mechanisms","Is tranylcypromine a hydrazine or a non-hydrazine MAOI?","Non-hydrazine (phenelzine and isocarboxazid are hydrazines)","Tranylcypromine is chemically related to amphetamine, which gives it a stimulant effect; it is still an irreversible inhibitor."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Antidepressant mechanisms","What is norquetiapine, and why does it matter?","Quetiapine's active metabolite; it blocks noradrenaline reuptake","This action is thought to add to quetiapine's antidepressant effect in bipolar depression and as augmentation."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Antipsychotic receptor profiles","How does brexpiprazole differ from aripiprazole?","Both are D2 partial agonists, but brexpiprazole has lower intrinsic activity at D2","It activates the receptor less, which is thought to cause less akathisia and restlessness."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Antipsychotic receptor profiles","Why must lurasidone be taken with food?","Its absorption is much greater with food","Taken on an empty stomach, blood levels may be too low to work."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Antipsychotic receptor profiles","Why is asenapine given under the tongue?","If swallowed, almost all of it is destroyed by first-pass metabolism","Sublingual absorption bypasses the liver; patients should not eat or drink for 10 minutes afterwards."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Antipsychotic receptor profiles","Which second-generation antipsychotic is known for prolonging QTc more than most, and is contraindicated after a recent heart attack?","Ziprasidone","It is not marketed in the UK but is often examined because of its QTc effect."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Antipsychotic receptor profiles","How does pimavanserin work?","5-HT2A inverse agonist with no D2 blockade","Because it spares dopamine, it treats psychosis in Parkinson's disease without worsening movement; it is licensed in the US, not the UK."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Antipsychotic receptor profiles","What striatal D2 occupancy gives antipsychotic effect, and above what level do EPSEs become likely?","About 60 to 80% for effect; EPSEs above about 80%","PET studies show a window: below 60% there is little benefit, above 80% motor side effects and raised prolactin rise sharply."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Dopamine pathways","Blocking D2 receptors in which pathway gives the antipsychotic effect?","The mesolimbic pathway","Overactive dopamine here is linked to positive symptoms such as hallucinations and delusions."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Dopamine pathways","Blocking D2 receptors in the mesocortical pathway may worsen which symptoms?","Negative and cognitive symptoms","This pathway runs from the ventral tegmental area to the prefrontal cortex, where dopamine is already thought to be low in schizophrenia."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Dopamine pathways","Which receptor change is thought to underlie tardive dyskinesia?","Supersensitivity (up-regulation) of nigrostriatal D2 receptors after long-term blockade","Blocked receptors become more sensitive; this is why dyskinesia can appear or worsen when the dose is reduced."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Dopamine pathways","D2 blockade in the basal ganglia and hypothalamus is thought to contribute to which life-threatening reaction?","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Sudden dopamine blockade causes rigidity and loss of temperature control."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Receptor pharmacology terms","What is an inverse agonist?","A drug that binds the same receptor as the agonist but produces the opposite effect","It lowers the receptor's baseline activity, whereas a neutral antagonist simply blocks it."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Receptor pharmacology terms","What is a partial agonist?","A drug that activates a receptor but cannot produce the full maximal response, even at high doses (a ceiling effect)","When natural transmitter is high it acts like an antagonist; when it is low, like an agonist. Aripiprazole and buprenorphine are examples."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Receptor pharmacology terms","How does a non-competitive antagonist change the dose-response curve?","It lowers the maximum response, and more agonist cannot fully overcome it","It binds elsewhere or irreversibly; a competitive antagonist only shifts the curve to the right, keeping the same maximum."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Receptor pharmacology terms","Propranolol is a competitive antagonist at beta-adrenergic receptors. What does 'competitive' mean?","It competes with the agonist for the same site, so enough agonist can overcome it (surmountable)","The dose-response curve moves right but reaches the same maximum."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Receptor pharmacology terms","Ketamine and phencyclidine block NMDA receptors in which way?","Non-competitive (open-channel) antagonism","They bind inside the ion channel pore (the PCP site), not at the glutamate binding site."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Receptor pharmacology terms","What is a heteroreceptor?","A receptor on a neuron for a transmitter other than the neuron's own","For example, serotonin receptors on dopamine neurons; they let one transmitter system regulate another."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Receptor pharmacology terms","What type of receptor is the dopamine D2 receptor?","A G-protein-coupled receptor with seven transmembrane regions","It works through second messengers (it inhibits adenylyl cyclase), so it acts more slowly than an ion channel."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Receptor pharmacology terms","What type of receptor is the glucocorticoid receptor?","A nuclear (intracellular) receptor that acts as a transcription factor","Steroids cross the cell membrane and change gene expression, so their effects take hours to days."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Receptor pharmacology terms","What type of receptor is the GABA-A receptor?","A ligand-gated chloride channel made of five subunits","GABA opens the channel; benzodiazepines bind a separate allosteric site and make it open more often."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Receptor pharmacology terms","Tolerance to hallucinogens can appear after a single dose. Which receptor change explains this?","Down-regulation (desensitisation) of 5-HT2A receptors","LSD and psilocybin act mainly as 5-HT2A agonists, and these receptors desensitise quickly."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Receptor pharmacology terms","Stimulation of which serotonin receptor is linked to the therapeutic effect of antidepressants?","5-HT1A","Over weeks, raised serotonin desensitises 5-HT1A autoreceptors, so more serotonin is released; this may explain the delay in benefit."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Receptor pharmacology terms","Reduced GABA function explains which withdrawal states?","Benzodiazepine and alcohol withdrawal","Long-term use adapts GABA-A receptors; when the drug stops, inhibition is too low, causing anxiety, tremor and seizures."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Other drug mechanisms","How do amphetamines differ from methylphenidate at the nerve ending?","Amphetamines also release dopamine and noradrenaline from storage vesicles; methylphenidate only blocks reuptake","Amphetamines enter the neuron and reverse the transporters, pushing transmitter out."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Other drug mechanisms","How does topiramate work?","It blocks sodium channels, enhances GABA, blocks AMPA/kainate glutamate receptors and inhibits carbonic anhydrase","The carbonic anhydrase action explains its kidney stones and metabolic acidosis."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Other drug mechanisms","How does vigabatrin work?","It irreversibly inhibits GABA transaminase, so GABA builds up","The name is a clue: vi-GABA-T-rin. It can cause permanent visual field loss."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Other drug mechanisms","How does riluzole work?","It reduces glutamate release (by sodium channel blockade), with some NMDA antagonism","It slows motor neurone disease, based on the idea of glutamate excitotoxicity."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Other drug mechanisms","How does ramelteon work?","Melatonin MT1 and MT2 receptor agonist","It shortens the time to fall asleep and has little abuse potential; it is not licensed in the UK."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Other drug mechanisms","How does flumazenil work?","Competitive antagonist at the benzodiazepine site of the GABA-A receptor","It reverses benzodiazepine and Z-drug effects but is short-acting, so sedation can return."],
["Receptors and mechanisms","Other drug mechanisms","Zolpidem binds mainly to GABA-A receptors containing which subunit?","Alpha-1","Alpha-1 receptors mediate sedation, so zolpidem is mainly hypnotic, with less anxiolytic and muscle-relaxant effect."],
/* ---------- Adverse effects and syndromes ---------- */
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Mood stabiliser side effects","What is the commonest side effect of valproate?","Gut upset: nausea, vomiting, indigestion and diarrhoea","It is worst in the first month and with fast dose increases; taking it with food or using a modified-release form helps."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Mood stabiliser side effects","A 29-year-old woman on valproate notices her hair falling out. Is this a known effect?","Yes: hair loss is a recognised valproate side effect","It is usually temporary, and hair may grow back curlier."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Mood stabiliser side effects","Which blood count change can valproate cause?","Thrombocytopenia (low platelets)","It is dose-related; check the FBC before surgery and if there is bruising or bleeding."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Mood stabiliser side effects","Which endocrine problem in women is linked to valproate?","Polycystic ovaries with raised androgens","It can cause irregular periods, hirsutism and infertility, another reason to avoid it in young women."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Mood stabiliser side effects","A 34-year-old man on valproate becomes drowsy and confused, but his liver tests and valproate level are normal. Which blood test is needed?","Serum ammonia","Valproate can cause hyperammonaemic encephalopathy even when LFTs and drug levels are normal."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Mood stabiliser side effects","Which skin disease can lithium make worse?","Psoriasis","Lithium can trigger or worsen psoriasis and acne, sometimes enough to stop the drug."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Mood stabiliser side effects","Apart from hypothyroidism, which gland problem can long-term lithium cause?","Hyperparathyroidism with high calcium","This is why calcium is checked before and during treatment."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Mood stabiliser side effects","How does lithium cause polyuria?","It makes the collecting duct less responsive to ADH (nephrogenic diabetes insipidus)","Lithium enters collecting duct cells and reduces aquaporin-2 water channels, so urine cannot be concentrated."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Mood stabiliser side effects","Which ECG change is common, and usually harmless, on lithium?","T-wave flattening or inversion","It is benign and reversible; rarer, more serious effects include sinus node dysfunction and bradycardia."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Mood stabiliser side effects","Which clinical features suggest lithium toxicity?","Coarse tremor, unsteadiness, slurred speech, vomiting, diarrhoea and confusion; later seizures and reduced consciousness","Toxicity is usually seen above about 1.5 mmol/L but can occur within the usual range, especially in older people."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Mood stabiliser side effects","What is the clinical management of suspected lithium toxicity?","Stop lithium, check an urgent level and U&Es, give IV fluids and get medical help; haemodialysis in severe cases","Lithium is cleared only by the kidneys, so restoring fluid and kidney function is key; dialysis removes it directly."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Mood stabiliser side effects","A 47-year-old woman on lithium starts a low-salt diet. What happens to her lithium level?","It rises","The kidney handles lithium like sodium; when sodium is low, it reabsorbs more of both."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Mood stabiliser side effects","A 79-year-old man on carbamazepine becomes drowsy and confused. Which blood test is most useful?","Sodium (carbamazepine can cause hyponatraemia)","Carbamazepine has an ADH-like effect; the risk is greater in older people and with diuretics."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Mood stabiliser side effects","Which genetic test is advised before carbamazepine in people of Han Chinese or Thai origin?","HLA-B*1502","Carriers have a much higher risk of Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Mood stabiliser side effects","Which cognitive side effect is typical of topiramate?","Word-finding difficulty and slowed thinking","Topiramate often affects language and concentration, so many people cannot tolerate it."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Mood stabiliser side effects","Which anticonvulsant tends to cause weight loss?","Topiramate","It reduces appetite, so it is sometimes used off-label to offset antipsychotic weight gain."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Mood stabiliser side effects","What are the risk factors for serious lamotrigine rash?","Fast titration, exceeding the starting dose, and taking valproate at the same time","Most serious rashes appear in the first 2 to 8 weeks; slow titration lowers the risk."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Mood stabiliser side effects","A 40-year-old woman stopped lamotrigine 2 weeks ago and wants to restart her usual dose. What should happen?","Restart at the starting dose and titrate up again","After a gap of more than about 5 half-lives, a full dose carries the same rash risk as a new start."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Antidepressant side effects","Why do SSRIs increase bleeding risk?","They deplete platelet serotonin, which platelets need to clump","Platelets cannot make serotonin and take it up through SERT. Risk rises with NSAIDs, aspirin, anticoagulants and older age."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Antidepressant side effects","A 76-year-old woman with a past peptic ulcer takes aspirin and needs an SSRI. What should be co-prescribed?","A proton pump inhibitor","SSRIs with antiplatelets or NSAIDs raise the risk of gut bleeding; a PPI protects the stomach."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Antidepressant side effects","Why should citalopram be used with caution after a recent heart attack?","It prolongs QTc in a dose-related way","Sertraline has the best safety evidence after myocardial infarction."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Antidepressant side effects","What is the maximum daily citalopram dose for people over 65?","20 mg","The MHRA set this in 2011 because of dose-related QTc prolongation (40 mg in younger adults)."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Antidepressant side effects","Which antidepressant causes dose-related high blood pressure, so blood pressure should be monitored?","Venlafaxine","At higher doses its noradrenergic action raises blood pressure, mainly diastolic."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Antidepressant side effects","Which class of antidepressant most reliably causes weight gain?","Tricyclic antidepressants","H1 and 5-HT2C blockade increase appetite; SNRIs and most SSRIs have little effect on weight."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Antidepressant side effects","A 44-year-old man on mirtazapine has a fever and sore throat. Which test?","Full blood count","Mirtazapine can rarely cause agranulocytosis; patients should report signs of infection."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Antidepressant side effects","Name two common side effects of MAOIs apart from dietary reactions.","Postural hypotension and insomnia (also ankle swelling and weight gain)","Postural hypotension is the commonest; phenelzine is notable for ankle oedema."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Antidepressant side effects","Which cardiac action of tricyclics explains a widened QRS?","Sodium channel blockade (a quinidine-like, class 1a effect)","This slows conduction; drugs with the same class 1a action make it worse."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Antidepressant side effects","A 27-year-old woman on amitriptyline has diarrhoea and collapses. The ECG shows polymorphic VT. What was probably on her earlier ECG?","A prolonged QTc (over 500 ms)","Diarrhoea lowers potassium and magnesium, which add to drug QTc prolongation and can trigger torsades de pointes."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Antidepressant side effects","A 76-year-old man stopped paroxetine 3 days ago. He has flu-like symptoms, dizziness and 'electric shock' feelings. Diagnosis?","Antidepressant discontinuation syndrome","Symptoms start within days of stopping; restarting the drug and tapering more slowly usually helps."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Antidepressant side effects","What is the clinical management of SSRI-induced hyponatraemia?","Stop or switch the SSRI, restrict fluids and monitor sodium; refer to the medical team if sodium is below 125 mmol/L or symptoms are severe","Mild cases settle with fluid restriction; severe or symptomatic cases need specialist medical care."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Antidepressant side effects","After SSRI-induced hyponatraemia, which antidepressants are lower-risk choices?","Less serotonergic drugs such as reboxetine, lofepramine or moclobemide","Serotonergic drugs are thought to carry the highest risk; still recheck sodium after starting."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Antidepressant side effects","Which drug can treat recurrent drug-induced SIADH when fluid restriction is not enough?","Demeclocycline","It blocks the action of ADH on the kidney, so more water is lost."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Antipsychotic side effects","Which antipsychotic is linked to pigmentary retinopathy?","Thioridazine","It occurs with high doses and long-term use and can cause permanent visual loss."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Antipsychotic side effects","A 50-year-old man on long-term chlorpromazine has blue-grey skin in sun-exposed areas. Cause?","Chlorpromazine pigmentation (with photosensitivity)","Chlorpromazine deposits in skin and eyes; advise sun protection."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Antipsychotic side effects","A 41-year-old man on risperidone has little or no ejaculate. Which receptor action is responsible?","Alpha-1 adrenergic blockade (retrograde or absent ejaculation)","Alpha-1 receptors close the bladder neck during ejaculation; when blocked, semen can pass backwards."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Antipsychotic side effects","Which impulse control problems are linked to aripiprazole?","Pathological gambling, compulsive shopping, binge eating and hypersexuality","Its partial agonist action at D2/D3 receptors may stimulate reward pathways; warn patients and families."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Antipsychotic side effects","Which mechanisms are thought to cause antipsychotic weight gain?","H1 and 5-HT2C blockade, raised prolactin and leptin resistance","5-HT2A blockade is not thought to cause weight gain."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Antipsychotic side effects","Which antipsychotics cause the most weight gain?","Clozapine and olanzapine","They have the strongest H1 and 5-HT2C blockade."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Antipsychotic side effects","Which factors increase the risk of tardive dyskinesia?","Older age, female sex, mood disorder, early EPSEs, longer exposure and first-generation drugs","Second-generation antipsychotics carry a lower, but not zero, risk."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Antipsychotic side effects","How soon does acute dystonia usually appear after starting an antipsychotic?","Within days: about half within 48 hours and 90% within 5 days","Young men and high-potency drugs carry the highest risk; IM or IV dosing can cause it within minutes to hours."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Antipsychotic side effects","How soon does tardive dystonia appear?","After months to years of treatment","Unlike acute dystonia it persists; options include botulinum toxin, switching to clozapine or deep brain stimulation."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Antipsychotic side effects","What is the first-line drug treatment for akathisia?","A low-dose beta-blocker such as propranolol","Other options are low-dose mirtazapine, a benzodiazepine or cyproheptadine; reducing or switching the antipsychotic helps most."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Antipsychotic side effects","Why is tetrabenazine not used for akathisia?","It depletes dopamine and can itself cause akathisia","Tetrabenazine is a VMAT2 inhibitor used for tardive dyskinesia and chorea."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Antipsychotic side effects","Which risk factors for QTc prolongation should you look for before starting an antipsychotic?","Heart disease, slow pulse, low potassium or magnesium, female sex, older age, anorexia nervosa and other QTc-prolonging drugs","Several risk factors together matter more than whether the drug is first- or second-generation."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Antipsychotic side effects","A 55-year-old man on quetiapine has a QTc of 470 ms. What should you do?","Consider reducing the dose or switching, repeat the ECG and consider cardiology advice","Between 440 ms (men) or 470 ms (women) and 500 ms, act to lower risk; above 500 ms stop the drug and refer."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Antipsychotic side effects","A 48-year-old man on an antipsychotic has a normal QTc but abnormal T-wave shape. What should you do?","Review the drug and get urgent cardiology advice","Abnormal T waves can signal repolarisation problems even when the QTc is normal."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Antipsychotic side effects","What is the treatment for neuroleptic malignant syndrome?","Stop the antipsychotic, give fluids and cooling, and transfer to medical care; benzodiazepines, and dantrolene or bromocriptine in severe cases","Dantrolene relaxes muscle; bromocriptine replaces lost dopamine activity."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Antipsychotic side effects","How does dantrolene work?","It blocks calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum in skeletal muscle","This relaxes muscle and reduces heat production; it was first used for malignant hyperthermia."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Antipsychotic side effects","A 36-year-old woman on clozapine for 9 months is admitted dehydrated with vomiting. She is confused and rigid, with a high CK. What should happen to clozapine?","Stop it: NMS is suspected","Dehydration is a risk factor for NMS; clozapine-related NMS can be atypical, with less rigidity."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Antipsychotic side effects","What are the features of anticholinergic toxicity?","Hot, dry, flushed skin, dilated pupils, fast pulse, urinary retention, absent bowel sounds and delirium","Remember: hot as a hare, dry as a bone, red as a beet, blind as a bat, mad as a hatter. Stop anticholinergic drugs and give supportive care."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Serotonin syndrome","Which criteria are used to diagnose serotonin syndrome, and what is the key sign?","The Hunter criteria; clonus (spontaneous, inducible or ocular)","Clonus with a serotonergic drug, plus agitation, sweating, tremor, hyperreflexia or high temperature, makes the diagnosis."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Serotonin syndrome","A 45-year-old woman on fluoxetine has lithium added. She develops tremor, sweating, fever, confusion and hyperreflexia. Diagnosis?","Serotonin syndrome","Lithium boosts serotonin function, so the combination can tip into toxicity."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Serotonin syndrome","What is the management of serotonin syndrome?","Stop serotonergic drugs, give supportive care with fluids and cooling, benzodiazepines for agitation, and cyproheptadine in moderate cases","Most cases settle within 24 hours; a very high temperature needs intensive care."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Clozapine side effects","A 34-year-old woman in her second week of clozapine has a temperature of 38°C. What should you check?","FBC, plus CRP and troponin","Benign fever is common early, but neutropenia, myocarditis, NMS and infection must be excluded."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Clozapine side effects","A 65-year-old man with a past seizure needs high-dose clozapine. What should be considered?","A preventive anticonvulsant, usually valproate (or lamotrigine)","Clozapine seizures are dose- and level-related; avoid carbamazepine because of agranulocytosis risk."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Clozapine side effects","Which drug is usually tried first for night-time drooling on clozapine?","Hyoscine hydrobromide (for example a 300 microgram tablet sucked at night)","It is an anticholinergic that reduces saliva; pirenzepine and glycopyrronium are alternatives."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Clozapine side effects","A 56-year-old woman starting clozapine has dizzy spells when getting out of bed. What is the main advice?","Get up slowly, and slow the titration if needed","Alpha-1 blockade causes postural hypotension, which is worst early in titration."],
["Adverse effects and syndromes","Clozapine side effects","A 34-year-old man on clozapine, valproate and levothyroxine has a white cell count below 3.0 x10^9/L. What is the immediate step?","Stop clozapine","A 'red' result means clozapine must stop at once, with daily blood counts until recovery."],
/* ---------- Interactions and switching ---------- */
["Interactions and switching","Enzyme inhibition","A 21-year-old man with asthma takes theophylline and needs an antidepressant. Which SSRI should be avoided?","Fluvoxamine","It strongly inhibits CYP1A2, which clears theophylline, so theophylline levels rise."],
["Interactions and switching","Enzyme inhibition","A 67-year-old man on warfarin after a stroke is depressed. Which SSRI carries the greatest interaction risk?","Fluvoxamine","It inhibits enzymes (including CYP2C9) that clear warfarin, raising the INR; all SSRIs also add bleeding risk."],
["Interactions and switching","Enzyme inhibition","A 33-year-old woman on diazepam is started on fluvoxamine. What happens?","Diazepam levels rise, causing more sedation","Fluvoxamine inhibits CYP2C19 and CYP3A4, which clear diazepam."],
["Interactions and switching","Enzyme inhibition","Which antibiotic inhibits CYP1A2 and can raise clozapine levels sharply?","Ciprofloxacin","Check clozapine levels and watch for sedation, hypotension and seizures."],
["Interactions and switching","Enzyme inhibition","Which SSRIs strongly inhibit CYP2D6?","Fluoxetine and paroxetine","They raise levels of tricyclics, risperidone, aripiprazole and some beta-blockers; the aripiprazole dose is often halved."],
["Interactions and switching","Enzyme inhibition","Which SSRIs have the fewest CYP450 interactions at usual doses?","Citalopram, escitalopram and sertraline","They are weak enzyme inhibitors, so they are often preferred in people on many medicines."],
["Interactions and switching","Enzyme induction","A 35-year-old man on quetiapine is started on carbamazepine. What happens to quetiapine?","Its level falls sharply","Carbamazepine induces CYP3A4, which breaks down quetiapine; aripiprazole and haloperidol are affected the same way."],
["Interactions and switching","Enzyme induction","Why should carbamazepine not be combined with clozapine?","Both can suppress the bone marrow, and carbamazepine lowers clozapine levels by enzyme induction","The combination increases agranulocytosis risk and makes clozapine less effective."],
["Interactions and switching","Enzyme induction","Which emergency contraception is preferred for a woman taking carbamazepine?","A copper intrauterine device","Enzyme induction makes hormonal emergency contraception less reliable; the copper IUD is unaffected."],
["Interactions and switching","Enzyme induction","A 44-year-old man stable on clozapine on a smoke-free ward is discharged and starts smoking 20 a day again. What happens to his clozapine level?","It falls, perhaps by about half, risking relapse","Hydrocarbons in smoke induce CYP1A2; the dose often needs increasing, guided by levels."],
["Interactions and switching","Enzyme induction","Does nicotine replacement therapy induce CYP1A2?","No: it is the tar (polycyclic hydrocarbons), not nicotine, that induces the enzyme","So switching from cigarettes to NRT or vaping still makes clozapine and olanzapine levels rise."],
["Interactions and switching","Enzyme induction","Which herbal remedy induces CYP3A4 and can make the pill, warfarin and many other drugs fail?","St John's wort","It also adds serotonergic effects, so it can cause serotonin syndrome with SSRIs."],
["Interactions and switching","Enzyme induction","Which antibiotics can make valproate levels fall sharply, risking seizures or relapse?","Carbapenems such as meropenem","The interaction is fast and large; another antibiotic is usually advised."],
["Interactions and switching","Enzyme induction","Why does the combined pill matter for a woman on lamotrigine?","Oestrogen roughly halves lamotrigine levels","In the pill-free week levels rise again; the dose may need changing when the pill is started or stopped."],
["Interactions and switching","Enzyme induction","A 30-year-old man on methadone starts rifampicin for TB. What may happen?","Opioid withdrawal, because rifampicin lowers methadone levels","Rifampicin, like carbamazepine and phenytoin, induces the enzymes that clear methadone."],
["Interactions and switching","Pharmacodynamic interactions","Why should IM olanzapine and IM lorazepam not be given close together?","Risk of excessive sedation, low blood pressure and slowed breathing","Leave at least an hour between them and monitor closely."],
["Interactions and switching","Pharmacodynamic interactions","Why must clomipramine never be combined with an MAOI?","It is strongly serotonergic and can cause serotonin syndrome","Clomipramine is the most serotonergic tricyclic, so the combination is contraindicated."],
["Interactions and switching","Pharmacodynamic interactions","Which over-the-counter decongestant can cause a hypertensive crisis with an MAOI?","Pseudoephedrine (and other sympathomimetics such as phenylephrine)","They release noradrenaline, which cannot be broken down while MAO is blocked."],
["Interactions and switching","Pharmacodynamic interactions","Why should opioids and benzodiazepines be combined only with great care?","Their sedative and breathing-suppressant effects add together","The MHRA has warned about this, and about opioids with gabapentin or pregabalin."],
["Interactions and switching","Pharmacodynamic interactions","A 50-year-old woman takes imipramine with chlorpromazine. Which side effects are likely to be worse?","Anticholinergic effects such as dry mouth and constipation","Both block muscarinic receptors, so their effects add up."],
["Interactions and switching","Pharmacodynamic interactions","Which antiemetic often prescribed on wards adds QTc risk when given with antipsychotics?","Ondansetron (also domperidone)","Combining QTc-prolonging drugs increases risk; check the ECG and electrolytes."],
["Interactions and switching","Pharmacodynamic interactions","Which diuretic class raises lithium levels the most?","Thiazides","They cause sodium loss, so the kidney holds on to lithium; loop diuretics have a smaller effect."],
["Interactions and switching","Pharmacodynamic interactions","What should a patient on disulfiram be told about hidden alcohol?","Avoid alcohol in mouthwash, cough medicines, some foods, and even hand gels or aftershave","Small amounts can trigger a reaction, and the risk lasts up to about two weeks after stopping disulfiram."],
["Interactions and switching","Pharmacodynamic interactions","A 42-year-old man on naltrexone needs pain relief after a fracture. What problem arises?","Opioid painkillers will not work at normal doses","Naltrexone blocks mu receptors for a day or more; use non-opioid pain relief with specialist advice."],
["Interactions and switching","Switching antidepressants","A 45-year-old woman on paroxetine for 4 weeks wants to switch to citalopram. How?","A direct switch is usually possible","Both are SSRIs, so stopping one and starting the other the next day keeps serotonin cover."],
["Interactions and switching","Switching antidepressants","A 46-year-old man on tranylcypromine wants to change to sertraline. How long must he wait?","2 weeks after stopping the MAOI","MAO enzyme takes about 2 weeks to regenerate after an irreversible MAOI."],
["Interactions and switching","Switching antidepressants","A 52-year-old woman on venlafaxine has insomnia and wants to switch to mirtazapine. How?","Cross-taper cautiously","Reduce venlafaxine while starting mirtazapine; watch for serotonin effects and venlafaxine discontinuation symptoms."],
["Interactions and switching","Switching antidepressants","How long after stopping moclobemide can another antidepressant be started?","About 24 hours","Moclobemide is short-acting and reversible, so MAO activity recovers within a day."],
["Interactions and switching","Switching antipsychotics","What are the main risks of stopping or switching antipsychotics abruptly?","Cholinergic rebound, withdrawal dyskinesia, rebound psychosis and relapse","A gradual cross-taper reduces these risks."],
["Interactions and switching","Switching antipsychotics","A 30-year-old man stops olanzapine suddenly and feels sick, sweaty and restless. Why?","Cholinergic rebound after stopping an anticholinergic antipsychotic","Muscarinic receptors up-regulate on olanzapine or clozapine, so sudden withdrawal causes rebound."],
["Interactions and switching","Switching antipsychotics","Stopping clozapine suddenly carries a particular risk of what?","Rapid rebound psychosis (and cholinergic rebound)","Taper over 1 to 2 weeks if possible, unless it must stop at once, for example for a red blood result."],
/* ---------- Special populations ---------- */
["Special populations","Pregnancy","What did the MHRA decide in 2024 about starting valproate in people under 55?","It should not be started unless two specialists independently agree there is no other effective or tolerated option","This applies to men and women; women who could become pregnant also need the Pregnancy Prevention Programme."],
["Special populations","Pregnancy","What are the risks to a baby exposed to valproate in the womb?","About 1 in 10 have a major malformation, and up to 30 to 40% have neurodevelopmental problems","These include neural tube defects, cleft palate, heart and limb defects, lower IQ and autism; risk rises with dose."],
["Special populations","Pregnancy","If lithium continues in pregnancy, how often should levels be checked?","Every 4 weeks, then weekly from 36 weeks, and within 24 hours of birth","Lithium clearance rises in pregnancy so levels fall; after delivery clearance drops quickly and levels can rise into the toxic range."],
["Special populations","Pregnancy","What does current evidence say about lithium and Ebstein's anomaly?","The risk is smaller than once thought, and part of it may relate to maternal mental illness itself","Ebstein's anomaly affects the tricuspid valve; after first-trimester exposure, a detailed fetal heart scan is offered."],
["Special populations","Pregnancy","A 25-year-old woman with bipolar disorder is pregnant for the first time and has a mixed episode. Which drug class is usually preferred?","An antipsychotic (for example haloperidol, olanzapine or quetiapine)","Antipsychotics have a larger reproductive safety evidence base than mood stabilisers; valproate and carbamazepine are avoided."],
["Special populations","Pregnancy","A 28-year-old pregnant woman takes olanzapine. Which extra test does she need?","Screening for gestational diabetes (an oral glucose tolerance test)","Antipsychotics, especially olanzapine and clozapine, raise the risk of gestational diabetes and weight gain."],
["Special populations","Pregnancy","Which SSRI is linked to heart defects after first-trimester exposure?","Paroxetine","Studies suggest a small rise in cardiac malformations, so it is not usually started in pregnancy."],
["Special populations","Pregnancy","What is poor neonatal adaptation syndrome?","Jitteriness, irritability, poor feeding and breathing problems in babies exposed to SSRIs late in pregnancy","It is usually mild and settles within about two weeks; it is commonest with paroxetine."],
["Special populations","Pregnancy","Which birth defect has been linked to first-trimester benzodiazepines?","Cleft lip and palate (the link is weak and uncertain)","Later in pregnancy the bigger concerns are neonatal withdrawal and floppy infant syndrome."],
["Special populations","Pregnancy","What folic acid dose is advised for a woman on an anticonvulsant mood stabiliser who might become pregnant?","5 mg a day","High-dose folic acid is advised before conception and in the first trimester, though it does not remove valproate's risks."],
["Special populations","Pregnancy","Why do lamotrigine levels need watching in pregnancy?","Levels fall, often by half or more, as liver glucuronidation increases","Seizure or mood relapse can follow; levels then rise quickly after delivery."],
["Special populations","Breastfeeding","Which mood stabiliser is usually avoided when breastfeeding?","Lithium","It passes freely into milk, and the baby's kidneys clear it slowly, so the infant can become toxic."],
["Special populations","Breastfeeding","Why is clozapine avoided when breastfeeding?","It passes into milk and may cause agranulocytosis and sedation in the baby","The infant cannot be monitored safely, so other antipsychotics are preferred."],
["Special populations","Breastfeeding","Which antipsychotics are commonly used when breastfeeding?","Olanzapine or quetiapine","They reach low levels in milk; watch the baby for sedation and poor feeding."],
["Special populations","Older adults","Why should anticholinergic burden be kept low in older people?","It increases confusion, falls, delirium and possibly long-term cognitive decline","Scales such as the ACB help; amitriptyline, paroxetine, oxybutynin and procyclidine all add to the total."],
["Special populations","Older adults","An 83-year-old woman becomes constipated and dizzy and falls after starting an antidepressant. Which drug is the likely cause?","Amitriptyline","Anticholinergic action causes constipation; alpha-1 blockade causes postural hypotension and falls."],
["Special populations","Older adults","What lithium level is usually aimed for in older adults?","Lower, around 0.4 to 0.7 mmol/L","Older people clear lithium more slowly and are more sensitive to its effects."],
["Special populations","Older adults","Which antipsychotic is licensed for short-term treatment of persistent aggression in Alzheimer's dementia?","Risperidone (for up to 6 weeks)","It is used only when there is a risk of harm and non-drug approaches have failed, because of the stroke and death risk."],
["Special populations","Older adults","Should antidepressants be routinely offered for mild to moderate depression in mild to moderate dementia?","No (NICE), unless there is a pre-existing severe mental illness","Trials such as HTA-SADD found sertraline and mirtazapine no better than placebo, with more side effects."],
["Special populations","Older adults","Why are benzodiazepines and Z-drugs risky in older people?","Falls and hip fractures, confusion and next-day sedation","Slower clearance lets these drugs build up."],
["Special populations","Children and young people","Which antidepressant is first-line for moderate to severe depression in under-18s?","Fluoxetine, given with psychological therapy","It has the clearest evidence of benefit over placebo; sertraline or citalopram are second-line."],
["Special populations","Children and young people","Why are young people seen often in the first weeks after starting an antidepressant?","Antidepressants can increase suicidal thinking and behaviour in under-25s early in treatment","Close follow-up, often weekly at first, is advised."],
["Special populations","Children and young people","Which antidepressants should not be used in under-18s because harms outweigh benefits?","Paroxetine, venlafaxine and tricyclics","Trials showed no clear benefit and more adverse effects."],
["Special populations","Children and young people","Which antipsychotic is licensed for persistent aggression in conduct disorder in children aged 5 and over?","Risperidone (short-term, up to 6 weeks)","It is a specialist option alongside parent training, licensed for children with intellectual disability."],
["Special populations","Children and young people","From what age is aripiprazole licensed for schizophrenia in the UK?","15 years","For manic episodes in bipolar I disorder it is licensed from 13 years, for up to 12 weeks."],
["Special populations","Children and young people","Which melatonin product is licensed for insomnia in children with autism?","Prolonged-release paediatric melatonin (Slenyto)","It is licensed from 2 to 18 years in autism or Smith-Magenis syndrome when sleep hygiene has not worked."],
["Special populations","Renal and hepatic impairment","Which antipsychotic needs the biggest dose reduction in renal impairment?","Amisulpride (also sulpiride)","Both are cleared unchanged by the kidneys, so they build up when kidney function falls."],
["Special populations","Renal and hepatic impairment","Which anticonvulsants used in psychiatry need dose reduction in renal impairment?","Gabapentin and pregabalin","They are excreted unchanged in urine, so they accumulate and cause sedation and dizziness."],
["Special populations","Renal and hepatic impairment","Which dementia drug needs a lower dose in moderate to severe renal impairment?","Memantine","It is mainly excreted unchanged by the kidneys."],
["Special populations","Renal and hepatic impairment","Which antidepressants are contraindicated in liver impairment?","Duloxetine and agomelatine","Both depend on the liver for clearance and can themselves injure it."],
["Special populations","Renal and hepatic impairment","Which alcohol relapse-prevention drug is cleared by the kidneys and avoided in renal impairment?","Acamprosate","It is not metabolised by the liver, so it can be used in mild to moderate liver disease, but not when kidney function is poor."],
/* ---------- Monitoring ---------- */
["Monitoring","Lithium","How often should lithium levels be checked once stable?","Every 3 months for the first year, then every 6 months","Keep 3-monthly checks for older people, those on interacting drugs, those with kidney or thyroid problems, or poor adherence."],
["Monitoring","Lithium","Which tests are repeated every 6 months on lithium?","Kidney function (U&Es and eGFR), thyroid function and calcium, plus weight","Lithium can harm the kidneys, thyroid and parathyroid glands over time."],
["Monitoring","Lithium","Why should lithium be prescribed by brand?","Brands differ in bioavailability, so switching can change the level","If a brand change cannot be avoided, recheck the level after 5 to 7 days."],
["Monitoring","Lithium","How should lithium be stopped?","Gradually, over at least 4 weeks (ideally up to 3 months)","Stopping suddenly raises the risk of rebound mania."],
["Monitoring","Lithium","A 39-year-old woman on lithium has vomiting and diarrhoea. What should she do?","Seek medical advice; lithium may need pausing and a level checked","Fluid loss reduces lithium clearance and can quickly cause toxicity."],
["Monitoring","Lithium","What practical advice should patients starting lithium receive?","Drink enough fluid, keep salt intake steady, avoid over-the-counter NSAIDs and know the signs of toxicity","Written information and a lithium record book are given."],
["Monitoring","Clozapine","Which baseline tests are needed before clozapine?","FBC with normal neutrophils, ECG, blood pressure, pulse, temperature, weight, glucose or HbA1c, lipids, and troponin and CRP","These detect neutropenia and heart risk and give values for later comparison."],
["Monitoring","Clozapine","How is myocarditis watched for in the first month of clozapine?","Troponin and CRP weekly for 4 weeks, with daily pulse, temperature and blood pressure","Most cases occur in the first 4 to 8 weeks; rising troponin or CRP with a fast pulse needs urgent review."],
["Monitoring","Clozapine","What defines a 'red' result on UK clozapine monitoring?","White cell count below 3.0 x10^9/L or neutrophils below 1.5 x10^9/L","Clozapine must stop at once, with daily blood counts until recovery."],
["Monitoring","Clozapine","What defines an 'amber' result on clozapine monitoring, and what happens?","White cells 3.0 to 3.5 or neutrophils 1.5 to 2.0 x10^9/L; clozapine continues with twice-weekly blood tests","It is a warning zone, watched closely until counts return to green."],
["Monitoring","Clozapine","What is benign ethnic neutropenia, and why does it matter for clozapine?","A naturally lower neutrophil count, common in people of African or Middle Eastern ancestry (Duffy-null)","With haematology advice, patients can be monitored against lower thresholds, so they are not wrongly denied clozapine."],
["Monitoring","Clozapine","When is a clozapine level taken, and when is it useful?","A trough level, about 12 hours after the last dose; useful for poor response, suspected toxicity, smoking changes or doubtful adherence","Levels vary greatly between people on the same dose, so the dose alone is a poor guide."],
["Monitoring","Valproate and carbamazepine","What is the usual target plasma range for valproate?","50 to 100 mg/L (some people with mania need up to 125 mg/L)","Use a trough sample; levels mainly help check adherence and toxicity."],
["Monitoring","Valproate and carbamazepine","Which tests are needed before and during valproate treatment?","LFTs and FBC at baseline and during the first 6 months, plus weight","Liver damage and low platelets mostly occur early in treatment."],
["Monitoring","Valproate and carbamazepine","What carbamazepine level is usually aimed for in mania?","Above about 7 mg/L (usual range about 4 to 12 mg/L)","Because carbamazepine induces its own breakdown, levels fall in the first weeks and need rechecking."],
["Monitoring","Other drugs","Which antidepressant needs liver function tests at baseline and around 3, 6, 12 and 24 weeks?","Agomelatine","It can raise transaminases and rarely cause liver failure; stop if they exceed three times the upper limit."],
["Monitoring","Other drugs","An 80-year-old woman is starting an SSRI. Which blood test should be checked early?","Sodium, at baseline and after 2 to 4 weeks","Hyponatraemia usually appears in the first month, and older age is the biggest risk factor."],
["Monitoring","Antipsychotic physical health","When does NICE advise an ECG before starting an antipsychotic?","If the drug's licence requires it, if there is heart disease or cardiac risk, or on inpatient admission","Recheck QTc after dose increases or adding interacting drugs."],
["Monitoring","Antipsychotic physical health","Why measure prolactin before starting an antipsychotic?","To have a baseline, so later symptoms such as galactorrhoea can be interpreted","Prolactin can also rise with pregnancy, hypothyroidism, stress and other drugs."],
["Monitoring","Antipsychotic physical health","Which drug has the best evidence for reducing antipsychotic-induced weight gain?","Metformin","It improves insulin sensitivity; lifestyle advice and switching to a lower-risk drug are also used."],
["Monitoring","Antipsychotic physical health","By how much is life expectancy reduced in severe mental illness, and why?","About 15 to 20 years, mostly from cardiovascular disease","Smoking, weight gain, diabetes and poorer access to care all contribute; annual physical health checks are advised."],
["Monitoring","Antipsychotic physical health","What is the Lester tool?","A UK framework (the Positive Cardiometabolic Health Resource) for acting on smoking, weight, blood pressure, glucose and lipids in people on antipsychotics","It sets thresholds for action, such as weight gain of more than 5% within 3 months of starting."],
["Monitoring","ADHD medication","What should be checked before starting ADHD medication?","Pulse, blood pressure, height, weight and a cardiac history (ECG only if there is a heart concern)","Stimulants raise pulse and blood pressure and can reduce appetite and growth."],
["Monitoring","ADHD medication","How often are height and weight checked in children on ADHD medication?","Height every 6 months; weight every 3 to 6 months","Plot on growth charts; a planned break from medication may be considered if growth slows."],
/* ---------- Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs ---------- */
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Opioid dependence","Which drugs are first-line for opioid detoxification and maintenance (NICE)?","Methadone or buprenorphine","Lofexidine is considered when the person chooses not to use an opioid-based detoxification."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Opioid dependence","Why are methadone doses increased slowly at the start of treatment?","Its long, variable half-life means levels keep rising for several days after each increase","Sedation can appear late, so increases are spaced out with regular review."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Opioid dependence","Which cardiac effect of methadone needs ECG monitoring?","QTc prolongation","Check the ECG at higher doses, or when there are other risk factors or QTc-prolonging drugs."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Opioid dependence","Why is supervised consumption used early in methadone treatment?","To make sure each dose is taken as prescribed and not diverted while the dose is being set","Supervision is relaxed as the person becomes stable."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Opioid dependence","A 32-year-old man who uses heroin wants to start buprenorphine. When should the first dose be given?","Once he has clear objective withdrawal signs, usually at least 8 to 12 hours after his last heroin","Given too early, buprenorphine displaces heroin and causes precipitated withdrawal."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Opioid dependence","Why is moving from methadone to buprenorphine difficult?","Methadone lasts so long that buprenorphine can precipitate withdrawal","The methadone dose is usually reduced first and a longer gap left (often 24 to 48 hours) before the first buprenorphine dose."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Opioid dependence","Why is naloxone combined with buprenorphine in some sublingual tablets?","To deter injection: naloxone is poorly absorbed under the tongue but blocks opioid effects if injected","Taken as prescribed, the naloxone has little effect."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Opioid dependence","A 23-year-old man on methadone is found drowsy with pinpoint pupils and slow breathing. What is the antidote?","Naloxone","It is a short-acting opioid antagonist; it can wear off before methadone does, so observe and repeat doses."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Opioid dependence","What are the signs of opioid withdrawal?","Dilated pupils, yawning, runny nose and eyes, goosebumps, sweating, cramps, diarrhoea and restlessness","The Clinical Opiate Withdrawal Scale (COWS) scores these objectively."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Opioid dependence","Before starting naltrexone for opioid relapse prevention, what must be confirmed?","That the person is opioid-free (usually for 7 to 10 days), for example by urine test or naloxone challenge","Naltrexone will precipitate withdrawal if opioids are still present."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Alcohol","Which drugs does NICE recommend first-line for alcohol relapse prevention after detox?","Acamprosate or oral naltrexone (disulfiram if these are unsuitable or the person prefers it)","They are combined with psychosocial treatment and usually given for up to 6 months."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Alcohol","When should acamprosate be started?","As soon as possible after withdrawal, and continued even if the person lapses","It reduces craving and, unlike disulfiram, causes no reaction if drinking restarts."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Alcohol","How long after the last drink should disulfiram be started?","At least 24 hours","Starting earlier can cause a reaction with alcohol still in the blood."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Alcohol","What happens if someone on disulfiram drinks alcohol?","Flushing, headache, nausea, vomiting, palpitations and a fall in blood pressure","Acetaldehyde builds up; severe reactions may need hospital care."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Alcohol","Why can disulfiram cause psychosis?","It inhibits dopamine beta-hydroxylase, so dopamine rises","This is why it is used with caution in people with a history of psychosis."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Alcohol","Which conditions contraindicate disulfiram?","Heart failure, coronary disease, previous stroke, high blood pressure, psychosis and severe personality disorder","The alcohol reaction puts strain on the heart and circulation."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Alcohol","What is nalmefene used for?","Reducing drinking (taken as needed) in alcohol dependence with a high drinking-risk level","It is an opioid modulator: a mu and delta antagonist and kappa partial agonist."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Alcohol","Which drug does NICE suggest for alcohol withdrawal in most people?","Chlordiazepoxide (carbamazepine is an alternative)","A long-acting benzodiazepine gives smooth cover; use a fixed or symptom-triggered regime, for example with CIWA-Ar."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Alcohol","What is the first-line treatment for delirium tremens?","Oral lorazepam (parenteral lorazepam, haloperidol or olanzapine if symptoms persist or oral is refused)","Treat in hospital and look for Wernicke's encephalopathy, infection and other causes."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Alcohol","Which drug should NOT be used for alcohol withdrawal seizures?","Phenytoin","It does not treat withdrawal seizures; use a quick-acting benzodiazepine such as lorazepam."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Alcohol","How should suspected Wernicke's encephalopathy be treated?","High-dose parenteral thiamine (for example Pabrinex), given before any glucose","Glucose without thiamine uses up what thiamine is left and can worsen brain damage."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Other substances","When should someone start varenicline?","1 to 2 weeks before the quit date, for a 12-week course","Nausea is the commonest side effect; the EAGLES trial did not show a significant rise in neuropsychiatric events."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Other substances","When is bupropion contraindicated for smoking cessation?","Epilepsy or seizure risk, eating disorders, a history of bipolar disorder, and during alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal","It lowers the seizure threshold and can trigger mania."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Other substances","Is there a licensed drug treatment for cocaine dependence?","No","NICE recommends psychosocial treatment such as contingency management."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Other substances","How is benzodiazepine dependence usually treated?","Switch to an equivalent dose of diazepam and taper slowly over weeks to months","Diazepam's long half-life smooths levels; small reductions every 1 to 2 weeks are typical."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Other substances","A 24-year-old man dependent on GBL is admitted to hospital. What is the main risk if he stops suddenly?","Rapid, severe withdrawal with agitation, delirium and seizures","It starts within hours; treatment is high-dose benzodiazepines in hospital, sometimes with baclofen."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Other substances","A 19-year-old woman on the PICU is drowsy with slow breathing after IM lorazepam. What can reverse it?","Flumazenil","Give oxygen and support breathing; flumazenil can trigger seizures in long-term benzodiazepine users, so use it carefully."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Dementia drugs","What does NICE recommend for mild to moderate Alzheimer's disease?","An acetylcholinesterase inhibitor: donepezil, galantamine or rivastigmine","Memantine is used in moderate disease if these are not tolerated, and in severe disease."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Dementia drugs","A 78-year-old man in the memory clinic has moderate Alzheimer's disease and already takes donepezil. What may be added?","Memantine","NICE advises considering it in moderate disease and offering it in severe disease."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Dementia drugs","What does NICE recommend for dementia with Lewy bodies?","Donepezil or rivastigmine (galantamine only if these are not tolerated)","Cholinergic loss is marked in DLB, so response is often good; memantine is an option if these fail."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Dementia drugs","Which acetylcholinesterase inhibitor is licensed in the UK for Parkinson's disease dementia?","Rivastigmine","It may slightly worsen tremor but improves cognition and hallucinations."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Dementia drugs","Should acetylcholinesterase inhibitors be used in frontotemporal dementia?","No","They do not help and may make behaviour worse."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Dementia drugs","Are acetylcholinesterase inhibitors recommended for pure vascular dementia?","No, only when it is mixed with Alzheimer's, Lewy body or Parkinson's disease dementia","Evidence of benefit in vascular dementia alone is weak."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Dementia drugs","An 81-year-old woman on galantamine has dizzy spells and a pulse of 44. What is the likely cause?","Bradycardia from the vagal (cholinergic) effect of the acetylcholinesterase inhibitor","Check the pulse before and during treatment; take care with sick sinus syndrome or heart block."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Dementia drugs","Why does the rivastigmine patch cause fewer side effects than the capsules?","It gives steadier blood levels with lower peaks","Nausea and vomiting are the main dose-limiting side effects of oral rivastigmine."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Dementia drugs","What is ARIA, and with which drugs is it seen?","Amyloid-related imaging abnormalities (brain swelling or microbleeds on MRI) with anti-amyloid antibodies such as lecanemab and donanemab","Risk is highest in APOE e4 carriers, especially those with two copies, so MRI monitoring is required."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Dementia drugs","Which gene is the main genetic risk factor for late-onset Alzheimer's disease?","APOE e4","One copy raises risk about three-fold, and two copies much more."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","Dementia drugs","Does NICE recommend ginkgo biloba for dementia?","No","NICE advises against herbal remedies for dementia because there is no good evidence of benefit."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","ADHD drugs","What is the first-line drug for ADHD in children aged 5 and over (NICE)?","Methylphenidate","If a 6-week trial at an adequate dose is not enough, switch to lisdexamfetamine."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","ADHD drugs","What is first-line medication for ADHD in adults (NICE)?","Lisdexamfetamine or methylphenidate","Atomoxetine is an option if these do not work or are not tolerated."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","ADHD drugs","When are atomoxetine or guanfacine used for ADHD?","When stimulants have not worked or are not tolerated, or there is a risk of misuse","Atomoxetine is not a controlled drug and has little abuse potential."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","ADHD drugs","What warnings come with atomoxetine?","Suicidal thoughts (especially early on) and rare liver injury","Monitor mood, and stop if jaundice appears."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","ADHD drugs","Why must guanfacine be tapered rather than stopped suddenly?","Sudden stopping can cause rebound high blood pressure","It lowers blood pressure and pulse; monitor both and watch for sedation."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","ADHD drugs","An 11-year-old boy on methylphenidate develops facial twitches and grunting noises. What has happened?","Stimulant-associated tics","Tics may appear or worsen; options are monitoring, a lower dose, or switching (for example to guanfacine or atomoxetine)."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","ADHD drugs","Which schedule of controlled drug are methylphenidate and lisdexamfetamine?","Schedule 2","Prescriptions must meet legal requirements, with no more than 30 days' supply advised."],
["Addiction, dementia and ADHD drugs","ADHD drugs","What is first-line for ADHD in children under 5?","An ADHD-focused parent training programme, not medication","Drugs are only considered after specialist review."],
/* ---------- Hypnotics and anxiolytics ---------- */
["Hypnotics and anxiolytics","Hypnotics","For how long should benzodiazepines or Z-drugs be prescribed for insomnia?","Short term only, usually no more than 2 to 4 weeks","Tolerance and dependence develop quickly; use the lowest dose, ideally on some nights only."],
["Hypnotics and anxiolytics","Hypnotics","What is the first-line treatment for chronic insomnia?","CBT for insomnia (CBT-I)","It works as well as hypnotics in the short term and better in the long term."],
["Hypnotics and anxiolytics","Hypnotics","How does daridorexant work, and when does NICE support its use?","A dual orexin receptor antagonist; for chronic insomnia (3 months or more) when CBT-I has been tried or is unsuitable","It blocks wake-promoting orexin signals; treatment is reviewed within 3 months."],
["Hypnotics and anxiolytics","Hypnotics","Which melatonin product is licensed in the UK for adult insomnia, and for whom?","Prolonged-release melatonin 2 mg (Circadin), for people aged 55 and over, for up to 13 weeks","It mimics the natural night-time rise in melatonin and carries little risk of dependence."],
["Hypnotics and anxiolytics","Hypnotics","Which taste side effect is common with zopiclone?","A bitter or metallic taste","The drug passes into saliva; it is a common reason for stopping."],
["Hypnotics and anxiolytics","Hypnotics","Which dangerous behaviours can Z-drugs cause?","Complex sleep behaviours such as sleepwalking, sleep-driving and eating while not fully awake","Stop the drug if these occur; risk is higher with alcohol or higher doses."],
["Hypnotics and anxiolytics","Hypnotics","Which chemical families do zolpidem and zaleplon belong to?","Zolpidem: imidazopyridine. Zaleplon: pyrazolopyrimidine","Like zopiclone, they are not benzodiazepines but act at the benzodiazepine site."],
["Hypnotics and anxiolytics","Hypnotics","Under UK drug-driving law, which psychotropic medicines have legal blood limits?","Several benzodiazepines, including diazepam, lorazepam, clonazepam, oxazepam and temazepam","People taking them as prescribed are not breaking the law if not impaired, but should be warned about driving."],
/* ---------- NICE first-line choices ---------- */
["NICE first-line choices","Anxiety disorders","Which drug does NICE suggest first for generalised anxiety disorder?","Sertraline","It is the most cost-effective SSRI; if it fails, try another SSRI or an SNRI, then pregabalin."],
["NICE first-line choices","Anxiety disorders","Should benzodiazepines be used for panic disorder?","No (NICE): they are linked to worse long-term outcomes","Offer an SSRI; if there is no improvement after 12 weeks, consider imipramine or clomipramine."],
["NICE first-line choices","Anxiety disorders","What drug treatment does NICE recommend for OCD?","An SSRI (any), or clomipramine","Higher doses and at least 12 weeks are often needed, with CBT including exposure and response prevention."],
["NICE first-line choices","Anxiety disorders","Which drugs can NICE suggest for adults with PTSD who prefer medication?","Venlafaxine or an SSRI such as sertraline","Trauma-focused psychological therapy is first-line; an antipsychotic such as risperidone may be added for disabling symptoms."],
["NICE first-line choices","Bipolar disorder","What does NICE recommend for acute mania in someone not already on an antimanic drug?","An antipsychotic: haloperidol, olanzapine, quetiapine or risperidone","If the first fails, try another; then consider adding lithium, then valproate (avoid valproate in women who could become pregnant)."],
["NICE first-line choices","Bipolar disorder","What does NICE recommend first for moderate to severe bipolar depression?","Fluoxetine with olanzapine, or quetiapine alone","If these fail, olanzapine alone or lamotrigine can be tried."],
["NICE first-line choices","Bipolar disorder","What is the first-line long-term treatment for bipolar disorder?","Lithium","If it is not effective enough, add valproate; if lithium is unsuitable, consider valproate or olanzapine alone."],
["NICE first-line choices","Depression and schizophrenia","A 50-year-old man has partly responded to venlafaxine, and switching has failed before. What augmentation does NICE suggest?","Lithium or an antipsychotic such as aripiprazole, quetiapine, olanzapine or risperidone","Adding a second drug suits people who tolerate their current antidepressant and have partly responded."],
["NICE first-line choices","Depression and schizophrenia","When does NICE say clozapine should be offered in schizophrenia?","After adequate trials of at least two different antipsychotics, one of them a non-clozapine second-generation drug","Each trial should last 6 to 8 weeks at an adequate dose before being called a failure."],
["NICE first-line choices","Depression and schizophrenia","Clozapine alone has not worked despite an adequate level. What does NICE suggest?","Add a second antipsychotic (for example amisulpride or aripiprazole) for 8 to 10 weeks","Choose one whose side effects do not overlap with clozapine's."],
["NICE first-line choices","Depression and schizophrenia","Which IM drugs does NICE recommend for rapid tranquillisation in adults?","IM lorazepam alone, or IM haloperidol combined with IM promethazine","Use lorazepam alone if the person has not had antipsychotics before, or if there is heart disease or no ECG."],
/* ---------- History and licensing ---------- */
["History and licensing","Pioneers","Who synthesised haloperidol, and when?","Paul Janssen, in 1958","It came from work on pethidine-like compounds and became the standard butyrophenone antipsychotic."],
["History and licensing","Pioneers","What was the first benzodiazepine, and who discovered it?","Chlordiazepoxide, by Leo Sternbach (marketed in 1960)","Diazepam followed in 1963."],
["History and licensing","Pioneers","Which was the first depot antipsychotic?","Fluphenazine (enanthate, then decanoate), in the 1960s","Depots improved adherence in long-term treatment."],
["History and licensing","Pioneers","Who first used camphor to induce seizures as a treatment, and who first used electricity?","Ladislas Meduna (camphor, 1934); Ugo Cerletti and Lucio Bini (electricity, 1938)","Meduna believed epilepsy and schizophrenia were antagonistic; the theory was wrong, but convulsive treatment helped."],
["History and licensing","Pioneers","Who first reported carbamazepine for mood disorders?","Takezaki and Hanaoka, in Japan (1971)","It is now mainly a second-line option in bipolar disorder."],
["History and licensing","Drug safety and law","Which disaster led to the UK Yellow Card scheme?","Thalidomide, which caused phocomelia (limb defects)","The Yellow Card scheme began in 1964 to collect reports of suspected adverse drug reactions."],
["History and licensing","Drug safety and law","What is phase IV of drug development?","Post-marketing surveillance after a licence is granted","It finds rare or long-term harms, for example sudden cardiac death with sertindole and blood problems with mianserin."],
["History and licensing","Drug safety and law","Which schedule of controlled drug are pregabalin and gabapentin in the UK?","Schedule 3 (since 2019)","They were reclassified because of rising misuse and dependence."],
["History and licensing","Drug safety and law","What is the difference between unlicensed and off-label prescribing?","Unlicensed: the drug has no UK licence. Off-label: a licensed drug used outside its licence (for example a different age, dose or indication)","Both are allowed with good evidence; the prescriber carries more responsibility and should explain and document the decision."],
]);
